1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,130
December 24th, 1999. Somewhere over Lucknow,

2
00:00:04,430 --> 00:00:09,470
1653 in the evening. A chief steward named Anil Sharma is serving

3
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:10,870
the captain his evening tea.

4
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:14,110
A masked man pushes past him,

5
00:00:14,310 --> 00:00:16,950
holding a revolver in one hand and a grenade in the other,

6
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:18,950
and forces his way into the cockpit.

7
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:22,150
The captain's name is Devi Sharan.

8
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:23,990
Without anyone in the cabin noticing,

9
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:25,610
he flips a single switch.

10
00:00:26,930 --> 00:00:29,250
It is the transponder code for hijack.

11
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:34,390
Three minutes later, air traffic control in Varanasi and Delhi both see it.

12
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:37,150
Indian Airlines Flight 814,

13
00:00:37,530 --> 00:00:40,590
a routine 75 -minute hop from Kathmandu to Delhi,

14
00:00:40,770 --> 00:00:42,490
is no longer a routine flight.

15
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:47,850
It will spend the next seven days on the ground in a country most of its own passengers

16
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:52,350
had never heard of. You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

17
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:56,190
India freed three terrorists to end this hijacking.

18
00:00:57,290 --> 00:01:00,410
Chapter 1, the airport that waved him through.

19
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:03,310
Tribhuvan International Airport,

20
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:06,430
Kathmandu. December 24th,

21
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:10,390
1999. A man walks through the metal detector.

22
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:13,530
It goes off. He walks through again.

23
00:01:14,110 --> 00:01:18,830
It goes off again. He is wearing a business class ticket and a nice jacket.

24
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:22,070
Nepalese police wave him through without a physical search.

25
00:01:22,930 --> 00:01:26,030
His name, investigators would later determine,

26
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:31,530
was Ibrahim Athar. Four more men board separately and regroup once the

27
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:34,550
plane is in the air. Five hijackers,

28
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:38,510
one flight attendant's chief steward already serving tea in first class,

29
00:01:38,990 --> 00:01:40,690
zero weapons found by security.

30
00:01:42,010 --> 00:01:43,510
The men called themselves Chief,

31
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:46,690
Doctor, Burger, Bowler and Shankar.

32
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:50,410
Investigators would later identify them as Ibrahim Athar,

33
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:53,470
Shahid Akhtar Saeed, Sunny Ahmed Kazi,

34
00:01:53,890 --> 00:01:57,170
Zaha Ibrahim Mystery and a man known only as Shakir.

35
00:01:58,670 --> 00:02:03,130
India's government would later link all five to a militant group called Harkat Ulmujahadeen.

36
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:05,850
Their demand was specific,

37
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:08,170
released three men from Indian prisons.

38
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:11,850
One of them was a Harkat ideologue named Masood Ajhar,

39
00:02:12,090 --> 00:02:17,510
held since 1994. A second was Mushtaq Ahmed Zargar.

40
00:02:18,070 --> 00:02:20,250
The third, Ahmed Omar Saeed Sheikh,

41
00:02:20,630 --> 00:02:21,950
had already tried once before.

42
00:02:22,270 --> 00:02:26,090
In 1994, kidnapping Western tourists to trade for Azhar.

43
00:02:26,430 --> 00:02:29,950
It failed. The timing was not an accident.

44
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:35,050
India had just fought a war in Kashmir that summer and pushed militant groups onto the

45
00:02:35,050 --> 00:02:40,250
back foot. Harkat Ulmujahadeen wanted something big enough to reverse that.

46
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:43,190
And three months before the hijacking,

47
00:02:43,630 --> 00:02:45,730
Indian intelligence had already received a warning.

48
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:49,030
A letter dated September 27th,

49
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:52,970
describing a plan to hijack a plane out of Kathmandu within two or three months.

50
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:56,690
Nothing came of it beyond a sealed court note.

51
00:02:57,210 --> 00:02:59,950
On December 24th, the warning became real.

52
00:03:01,590 --> 00:03:06,970
Chapter 2, 45 minutes. The hijackers ordered the plane to Amritsar,

53
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:09,110
in Indian territory, to refuel.

54
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:13,230
This is the moment India has the best chance to end it.

55
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:15,770
The plane lands at 604 in the evening.

56
00:03:16,230 --> 00:03:18,930
A National Security Guard commando team is put on alert.

57
00:03:20,310 --> 00:03:22,270
They do not reach the airport in time.

58
00:03:22,950 --> 00:03:24,310
Accounts differ on exactly why.

59
00:03:24,670 --> 00:03:28,010
A team stuck in traffic between a base outside Delhi and the airport.

60
00:03:28,470 --> 00:03:30,210
A decision chain that took too long.

61
00:03:30,710 --> 00:03:34,070
Local police left to stall for time with no equipment to storm a plane.

62
00:03:34,950 --> 00:03:40,050
A former top police officer in Punjab later said storming it with rifles and no assault

63
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:41,670
gear would have been futile.

64
00:03:42,590 --> 00:03:46,090
Critics call it a command and control failure that should never have happened.

65
00:03:47,470 --> 00:03:49,530
On the ground, refueling stalls.

66
00:03:50,230 --> 00:03:53,070
The hijackers grow furious and turn violent.

67
00:03:54,550 --> 00:03:55,710
Two passengers are stabbed.

68
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:58,630
One of them, a young man named Rupan Katyal,

69
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:02,530
does not survive. He was 25 years old,

70
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:03,810
returning from his honeymoon.

71
00:04:04,770 --> 00:04:08,150
At 747, 45 minutes after landing,

72
00:04:08,430 --> 00:04:10,210
with commandos still not in position,

73
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:12,670
the plane takes off again without clearance.

74
00:04:14,130 --> 00:04:17,290
It would never again be on the ground anywhere India controlled.

75
00:04:18,870 --> 00:04:21,710
Chapter 3. The bluff that got them into Pakistan.

76
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:24,610
The plane heads for Lahore.

77
00:04:25,210 --> 00:04:28,170
Pakistan, at first, refuses to let it land.

78
00:04:30,010 --> 00:04:31,470
Captain Shiran is running low on fuel,

79
00:04:31,670 --> 00:04:33,350
with no runway willing to take him.

80
00:04:34,310 --> 00:04:39,310
So he bluffs. He radios Lahore air traffic control and tells them if they don't clear

81
00:04:39,310 --> 00:04:41,950
him to land, he will put the plane down on a highway.

82
00:04:43,510 --> 00:04:47,390
Lahore grants clearance. The plane lands at 801 and refuels.

83
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:51,610
India's High Commissioner in Pakistan tries to reach the plane.

84
00:04:52,070 --> 00:04:54,370
He is delayed long enough that it doesn't matter.

85
00:04:55,710 --> 00:04:58,350
By 1032, it is airborne again,

86
00:04:58,530 --> 00:05:02,430
this time for Dubai. At 1 .32 in the morning,

87
00:05:02,810 --> 00:05:04,110
in the United Arab Emirates,

88
00:05:04,650 --> 00:05:07,090
the hijackers make their first and only concession.

89
00:05:08,730 --> 00:05:10,230
27 passengers are released,

90
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:12,030
so is Rupan Katyal's body.

91
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:16,070
By dawn, the plane is airborne one more time.

92
00:05:16,410 --> 00:05:20,390
Its next stop is a country with no functioning government India could even negotiate

93
00:05:20,390 --> 00:05:23,050
with directly. Chapter 4.

94
00:05:23,510 --> 00:05:26,470
Kandahar 8 .33 in the morning,

95
00:05:26,850 --> 00:05:32,110
December 25th. The plane lands at Kandahar airport in Taliban controlled Afghanistan.

96
00:05:33,750 --> 00:05:35,890
Armed Taliban fighters surround it on the tarmac.

97
00:05:37,310 --> 00:05:40,590
For the hostages inside, food and water become irregular.

98
00:05:40,930 --> 00:05:43,030
The standoff will last seven days.

99
00:05:44,470 --> 00:05:46,630
The Taliban's own foreign minister at the time,

100
00:05:47,090 --> 00:05:50,710
Wakil Ahmed Mutawakil, positioned his government as a mediator,

101
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:53,810
providing security around the aircraft and a channel for talk.

102
00:05:54,950 --> 00:05:58,870
India sent three of its most senior diplomats to negotiate in person,

103
00:05:59,430 --> 00:06:02,650
Vivek, Katju, Ajit Deval and C .D.

104
00:06:02,830 --> 00:06:06,630
Sahay. The hijackers' opening demand was enormous,

105
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:09,990
36 prisoners and $200 million.

106
00:06:11,810 --> 00:06:17,050
Over seven days, India's negotiators talked that number down to three names and no money.

107
00:06:18,050 --> 00:06:21,350
When it ended, the Taliban did not arrest the hijackers,

108
00:06:21,750 --> 00:06:26,470
as India had asked. They gave them roughly 10 hours to leave and drove them toward the

109
00:06:26,470 --> 00:06:30,750
Pakistan border. Analysts still argue about what that means.

110
00:06:31,210 --> 00:06:34,230
No government from that era exists anymore to answer for it.

111
00:06:35,610 --> 00:06:39,010
Chapter 5. The Decision Back in Delhi,

112
00:06:39,650 --> 00:06:42,090
India's government was not united on what to do.

113
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:44,790
Home Minister L. Kang, Advani,

114
00:06:44,870 --> 00:06:47,150
opposed releasing any prisoners at all.

115
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:50,710
External Affairs Minister Jaswant Singh led the talks,

116
00:06:51,210 --> 00:06:55,470
and personally flew to Kandahar with the three men when the decision was finally made.

117
00:06:56,990 --> 00:07:00,750
National Security Advisor Brajesh Mishra later said the decision was unanimous.

118
00:07:01,530 --> 00:07:06,250
Advani, years afterward, said he hadn't been told everything about Singh's personal role

119
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:09,630
on that flight. On December 31,

120
00:07:10,150 --> 00:07:13,090
1999, seven days after it began,

121
00:07:13,530 --> 00:07:16,810
the swap happened. Masood Ajhar,

122
00:07:17,250 --> 00:07:20,450
Mushtaq Ahmed Zargar, Ahmed Omar Saeed Shaikh,

123
00:07:20,690 --> 00:07:23,850
all three released. In exchange,

124
00:07:24,390 --> 00:07:26,270
every remaining hostage came home.

125
00:07:27,670 --> 00:07:30,890
Of the 190 people who boarded that flight in Kathmandu,

126
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:34,730
189 walked away. Rupan Katyal did not.

127
00:07:35,770 --> 00:07:39,610
The five men who had actually carried out the hijacking were never arrested.

128
00:07:40,090 --> 00:07:44,050
To this day, no government has confirmed capturing or killing any of them.

129
00:07:45,290 --> 00:07:50,530
Chapter 6. What Masood Ajhar did next Within weeks of walking free,

130
00:07:50,970 --> 00:07:52,830
Masood Ajhar founded a new organisation.

131
00:07:53,430 --> 00:07:55,110
He called it Jaish -e -Muhammad.

132
00:07:56,570 --> 00:08:01,170
In 2001, Jaish -e -Muhammad was formally linked by Indian authorities to an attack on

133
00:08:01,170 --> 00:08:02,470
the Indian parliament itself.

134
00:08:02,470 --> 00:08:05,770
Nine people were killed, along with all five attackers.

135
00:08:07,330 --> 00:08:12,110
In 2016, Indian investigators attributed an attack on the Pathankot Air Base to the same

136
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:16,090
organisation. Seven security personnel and one civilian died.

137
00:08:17,550 --> 00:08:20,870
In 2019, Jaish -e -Muhammad did something it rarely does.

138
00:08:21,250 --> 00:08:26,450
It publicly claimed responsibility itself on video for an attack in Palwama that killed

139
00:08:26,450 --> 00:08:31,550
40 security personnel. The organisation the hijackers set out to

140
00:08:31,550 --> 00:08:35,870
save was declared a banned terrorist group by Pakistan in 2002.

141
00:08:36,510 --> 00:08:39,330
By multiple accounts, it kept operating anyway.

142
00:08:40,850 --> 00:08:46,330
In May of 2025, Indian airstrikes hit a Jaish -e -Muhammad compound in Bahawalpur,

143
00:08:46,710 --> 00:08:49,790
Pakistan. Masood Ajhar's brother,

144
00:08:50,090 --> 00:08:54,690
Abdul Raufazhar, described by Indian sources as the planner of the original hijacking

145
00:08:54,690 --> 00:08:57,310
was killed. So it was his brother -in -law,

146
00:08:57,730 --> 00:09:02,730
Muhammad Yusuf Azhar. Masood Ajhar himself said ten members of his own

147
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:04,070
family were killed in the strikes,

148
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:07,130
including his sister, a nephew and a niece.

149
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:09,970
He said he had no regret.

150
00:09:10,550 --> 00:09:12,910
As of this recording, he is still alive.

151
00:09:14,370 --> 00:09:19,250
Chapter 7 Where Everyone Else Is Now Ahmed Omar Syed Sheikh,

152
00:09:19,670 --> 00:09:23,290
the second man released, was later convicted for a different crime entirely.

153
00:09:23,290 --> 00:09:27,570
The 2002 kidnapping and murder of journalist Daniel Pearl.

154
00:09:29,210 --> 00:09:32,370
A Pakistani court overturned that murder conviction in 2020.

155
00:09:32,990 --> 00:09:37,810
The country's Supreme Court upheld the acquittal in 2021 and finally dismissed the last

156
00:09:37,810 --> 00:09:39,410
appeals in January of 2024.

157
00:09:41,470 --> 00:09:43,010
Legally, he is a free man,

158
00:09:43,270 --> 00:09:46,590
but Pakistani authorities have repeatedly kept him in detention anyway,

159
00:09:46,870 --> 00:09:48,250
moving him between facilities.

160
00:09:48,690 --> 00:09:51,790
No confirmed report says he has actually returned to public life.

161
00:09:53,070 --> 00:09:55,910
Mushtaq Ahmed Zargar, the third man released,

162
00:09:56,170 --> 00:10:01,590
lives in Pakistan today. India's government formally designated him an individual terrorist

163
00:10:01,590 --> 00:10:06,630
in 2023. The five men who physically hijacked the plane were

164
00:10:06,630 --> 00:10:12,090
never identified as caught or killed by any government anywhere in 26 years.

165
00:10:13,670 --> 00:10:17,650
Captain Devi Sharan, the man who flipped a hijack switch without anyone noticing,

166
00:10:17,970 --> 00:10:19,990
kept flying for another 25 years.

167
00:10:19,990 --> 00:10:23,370
He took his final flight on January 4th,

168
00:10:23,370 --> 00:10:26,930
2025. Rupin Katyal's widow,

169
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:31,510
Rakhna, said publicly that no government official attended her husband's last rites.

170
00:10:33,010 --> 00:10:38,510
Chapter 8 The Show That Made India Furious In August of 2024,

171
00:10:38,990 --> 00:10:41,610
Netflix released a dramatised series about the hijacking.

172
00:10:41,930 --> 00:10:43,850
It set off a real political fight.

173
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:48,830
The controversy centred on two of the hijacker's codenames used on screen,

174
00:10:49,470 --> 00:10:52,190
Pola and Shankar. Both sound Hindu.

175
00:10:53,790 --> 00:10:55,390
Critics said using those names upfront,

176
00:10:55,790 --> 00:10:58,290
without immediately explaining who the men actually were,

177
00:10:58,430 --> 00:11:01,530
made it look like the hijacker's real identities were being hidden.

178
00:11:02,990 --> 00:11:05,190
Here is the part almost no one reported clearly.

179
00:11:05,530 --> 00:11:06,890
Those codenames were real.

180
00:11:07,250 --> 00:11:10,790
The hijackers actually used them in 1999 on the plane.

181
00:11:11,750 --> 00:11:13,770
Multiple fact -checks confirmed it.

182
00:11:13,990 --> 00:11:15,270
The show didn't invent anything.

183
00:11:15,590 --> 00:11:17,470
The argument was about what showing them first,

184
00:11:17,890 --> 00:11:19,550
without more context, implied.

185
00:11:21,210 --> 00:11:25,790
India's government summoned Netflix's own India content head over it on September 2nd.

186
00:11:26,950 --> 00:11:28,910
Netflix updated its opening disclaimer,

187
00:11:29,530 --> 00:11:32,390
adding the hijacker's real names alongside the codenames.

188
00:11:33,850 --> 00:11:38,410
Separately, a news agency sued the show's makers over how it used old footage.

189
00:11:38,810 --> 00:11:41,150
That case was still unresolved as of this recording.

190
00:11:42,670 --> 00:11:47,430
26 years later, Nepal's own government reviewed security at the airport this all started

191
00:11:47,430 --> 00:11:50,610
from. In November of 2025,

192
00:11:51,170 --> 00:11:55,750
a government committee found that security at Tribhuvan International Airport was still

193
00:11:55,750 --> 00:12:00,150
weak. The same corridor. A different generation of passengers.

194
00:12:00,650 --> 00:12:05,770
The same open question. If a seven -day hostage crisis could still be reopening

195
00:12:05,770 --> 00:12:08,830
arguments in a courtroom in a newsroom 26 years later,

196
00:12:09,250 --> 00:12:13,870
wait until you see what happened when four hijackers tried the exact same thing on an

197
00:12:13,870 --> 00:12:16,030
American plane called United 93,

198
00:12:16,250 --> 00:12:18,290
and the passengers didn't wait for a negotiation.

199
